   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The mesa is a solution humans have created, in which hardware and software implement features to
   solve a human problem.  The hardware and the software implementations are highly configurable.

   There are two views to the configuration of the mesa, or computer, on which SimpleSal is running.
   1) there must be references to interfaces of devices that cause the device to work in some manner
        a) what are the registers of a serial port device on this mesa that configure the baud rate?
        b) multiplexed interfaces are common in larger chips, the device itself has to be enabled.
   2) references by software for each device need to know where the registers exist on this mesa
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   GPIO interfaces of chipset mesas is the bread-and-butter money-maker for the chip manufacturer,
   so the interface can often by quite complex in both implementation and configuration choices.
   The more sets of useful configurations that can be created from one chip, the more customers.
   The demand for these interfaces compared to the pin requirements results in multiplexed pins,
   both at power-on due to fused pins controlling the purchased abilities, or, at run time when
   either one device OR another device (of many) is configured to be controlling the pin itself.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The values LOW and HIGH are conclusions by the mesa, choosing from 0 or 1.  Physical signal
   attributes are interpreted by the mesa after an electronic configuration is applied.  The
   interpretation by the electronic circuity as configured through the inclusion/exclusion of the
   electronic components affects the electronic signal reported by the Digital GPIO pin as 0 or 1.
   For example, inputs with a "pull up" configuration will report 1 for values detected as less
   1 by some amount.  This occurs within the chip, between the external pin and the processor.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Pins on Arduino shields line up with Arduino mesa Pins which are connected to true processor pins.
        "true mesa pin 12" means the pin on mesa labeled with "12" on the connector to the shield.
        "processor pin 22" means the pin on the processor as described by the mesa design documents.
   The tuple (mesa pin 12, processor pin 22) identifies one signal carrier between the processor
   and the device at the other end of the wire.  Arduino designs keep these two numbers the same;
   a complex chip with dozens of pins with configurations changeable in real time probably won't.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef int PinNumber_t;

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The idea of a pin is instantiated in every mesa.  The definition of the specific attributes of
   pins is at some level generic, within the context of the software supporting the mesa.  The
   definition of the mesa provided by the Vendor is almost always a requirement for implementing
   software for the mesa.  The definition includes #defines for bit fields, registers, meanings of
   bit fields (HIGH and LOW) that the Vendor asserts matches the physical attributes of the device.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   "Pin Mode" is a common concept based on electronics fundamentals; implementation may vary by Mesa
   with respect to the values used to configure each Mesa.  Creating an abstract symbol here, and
   using it in the software that is common to all Mesas, allows the focus of variation to be here.
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef enum PinMode_e


   The Uno is a good example because it has a variety of Mesas produced over the years, their
   own software developers have already created an abstraction that can be adopted and narrowed.
   #ifdef MESA_ONEOF_UNO_R7

